From a996f1632a9a717975eb106baf916b9f50b4be74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 18:26:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Multiplayer, first cut: deterministic lockstep over TCP with
 desync detection

Two players, host and joiner, both running the whole simulation and exchanging
only what the players DID. Nothing about the world crosses the wire, so traffic
is a few bytes a turn however many serfs are walking about - the only model that
works for a game this size, where one state snapshot is 2 MB.

It is viable because the simulation is deterministic, which I checked before
choosing: every random number in it comes from game.random_int() (the 3-word
RandomState port) and nothing in the simulation reads the clock. The only
irandom in the whole simulation layer seeds RandomState at construction, and the
host sends its seed so both start identical. The two places that do use irandom -
the "borntodie" effects and the panel blink - are cosmetic by deliberate design
and must STAY that way; a wall-clock value or an unseeded random reaching the
simulation shows up as a desync a thousand ticks later, not as a crash where it
happened.

Model: time is cut into turns of 5 ticks (100ms). A command issued during turn T
executes on turn T+2 on both machines, in the same order, local-then-peer as a
rule rather than by arrival time. A machine may not simulate turn T until the
peer's packet for T has arrived, and the packet is sent every turn even when
empty - silence has to mean "not yet", never "nothing to do". The two-turn delay
is what hides latency.

Desync detection: every ten turns both hash the world - serf positions, states,
animations and counters, buildings, flags, and the RNG state itself - and
compare. A mismatch stops the game and says so, rather than letting two players
play on in separate realities each believing they are winning. The hash folds
with 31 into a 31-bit accumulator on purpose: GML numbers are doubles, exact to
2^53, so an FNV-style 32-bit hash would silently lose its low bits the moment it
multiplied.

Two races closed while writing it, both of which would have looked like a hang:
- net_begin must NOT clear the turn maps. The host primes turns 0 and 1 and
  sends them with the start message, so on a local connection they arrive before
  the client has finished starting - clearing would discard them and the client
  would wait forever for a turn 0 that is never sent again. They are cleared in
  net_host/net_join, where nothing can have arrived yet.
- The host must not call set_player(0) after set_game, which has already
  selected player 0: the second call deletes the panel and then returns early on
  its unchanged-index test, leaving no panel at all. set_game's own comment
  describes that trap.

Speed is pinned to DEFAULT_GAME_SPEED while networked, because game_speed
changes how much simulation one tick does and the two must agree. It becomes a
command later.

Deliberately not here yet: a lobby (F7 hosts, F8 joins 127.0.0.1, so two
instances on one machine test it with no network at all), any command but
build-flag, AI slots, reconnection, and save/load of a networked game.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DFktm5o1kLnS2Pgvyp8tD4
